.. DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1612.07652 ... In particular, we define a no- tion of k-matroidal colorability of a graph, and prove a fractional version of a conjecture, that every graph G is 2∆(G)-matroidally colorable. ... En route, we give a survey of some recent developments in the theory of ISR’s.\n\n# Fair representation in the intersection of two matroids\n\n  * December 2016\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1612.07652\n\nAuthors:\n\nRon Aharoni\n\n  * Technion – Israel Institute of Technology\n\nEli Berger\n\n  * University of Haifa\n\nDaniel Kotlar\n\n  * Tel Hai Academic College\n\nRan Ziv\n\n  * Tel Hai Academic College\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a simplicial complex ${\\mathcal C}$ denote by $\\beta({\\mathcal C})$ the minimal number of edges from ${\\mathcal C}$ needed to cover the ground set. If ${\\mathcal C}$ is a matroid then for every partition $A_1, \\ldots, A_m$ of the ground set there exists a set $S \\in {\\mathcal C}$ meeting each $A_i$ in at least $\\frac{|A_i|}{\\beta({\\mathcal C})}$ elements. We conjecture that a slightly weaker result is true for the intersections of two matroids: if ${\\mathcal D}={\\mathcal P} \\cap {\\mathcal Q}$, where ${\\mathcal P},{\\mathcal Q}$ are matroids on the same ground set V and $\\beta({\\mathcal P}), \\beta({\\mathcal P}) \\le k$, then for every partition $A_1, \\ldots, A_m$ of the ground set there exists a set $S \\in {\\mathcal D}$ meeting each $A_i$ in at least $(\\frac{1}{k}-\\frac{1}{|V|})|A_i|-1$ elements. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFAIR REPRESENTATION IN THE INTERSECTION OF TWO MATROIDS · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/fair-representation-in-the-intersection-of-two-matroids-4)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: